  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 318 AM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe long term period will begin on Monday night with an upper ridge \nextending from the Deep South into southeast Canada. A surface high \nassociated with this ridge will also be moving from the eastern \nGreat Lakes towards the Northeast. Though low level moisture from \nthe Gulf will linger across Georgia (with dewpoints in the upper 60s \nto low 70s), subsidence of dry mid-level air under the ridge will \nlargely suppress convection on Tuesday afternoon, with only isolated \nthunderstorms anticipated in portions of east-central Georgia. \nUnderneath mostly clear skies, high temperatures are forecast to \nrise into the low 90s in all but the far northeastern mountains on \nTuesday afternoon. Late Tuesday into Wednesday, the surface high \nwill lead to the development of a CAD wedge along the lee side of \nthe Appalachian, which is expected to spread relatively cooler and \ndrier air into the eastern portions of Georgia. Low temperatures on \nWednesday and Thursday will start each morning in the 60s while high \ntemperatures in the afternoons will be largely in the upper 80s. \nWith the upper ridge lingering over the eastern CONUS, rain chances \nwill remain minimal across the forecast area on Wednesday and \nThursday. Chances for diurnally-driven showers and thunderstorms \nwill gradually increase starting on Friday and into next weekend as \nthe axis of the ridge begins to retrograde back to the west of the \narea.",
